Subject: Diagnostic: rollback inputs, replay and outgoing deterministic data
Upstream-base: 9046e67aa2077a7eb4ed6e28c9df3c1d3b368f84

Rollback study. Under RECHARGED_ROLLBACK_TEST the port's rollback (in the
game module) saves the match after every fixed step and goes back a few
steps to run them again. A replayed step must get exactly the inputs the
first run got, so the routers report what they apply before each step
(every machine's packed player inputs and the deterministic data events
they queue for the simulation, in their order), and ApplyRollbackInputs
applies a report again. FixedUpdateTask passes the port a replay step:
those inputs and the simulation, no pads, no capture, nothing sent.

The rollback restores the drawing's objects with the match, so the replay
step also runs, around the step and in Run's task order, what the frame's
other tasks do to the match and its presentation, without drawing, reading
pads or files, sending or making sounds: the particles' update, the front
end's presentation, HUD, scenes, lighting, models and stadium, the event
dispatch, then BeginFrameTask's presentation, NIS, mega blend and cameras.
The port gives each part the time step its frame first had. Replaying the
steps alone left all of it a little further behind at every rollback.

Deterministic data the simulation queues to send (player visibility, mega
strike results, NIS loaded) is an input of a later step on every machine:
a replayed step queues none, its data went out when it first ran. While it
waits in the router's queue it lives outside the game's arenas, which a
rollback restores while the router's queue goes on.

The rollback leaves the drawing's memory alone (the frame memory and the
resource pools with their memory): the port tracks its suballocations for
the host, and a pool restored to an earlier top would allocate over them.
glxMemory lists the live pools, and mscharged_rollback_graphics_memory
returns pointers into all of it. Without RECHARGED_ROLLBACK_TEST nothing
changes.

